      "content": "helped a bit but it still feels like you're giving both sides equal weight when one side is basically just trying to block progress. i mean, if the goal is to make cities livable and affordable for everyone, isn't the argument for preserving single-family neighborhoods just a way to maintain exclusivity and keep people out? it feels like the \"social fabric\" argument is just a polite way to say people don't want their neighborhoods to change. is there actually any legitimate economic or social reason to keep single-family zoning, or is it just about protecting the status quo of wealthy homeowners?",
